# These scripts are checked out on Windows and EXECUTED on Linux VMs, embedded
# verbatim into instance metadata by Terraform's file()/templatefile(). A CRLF
# line ending survives that path and reaches the guest, where `#!/usr/bin/env
# bash\r` is not an interpreter and every startup script fails at boot.
#
# Force LF regardless of the checkout platform's core.autocrlf.
#
# `.ps1` is here for a different reason, and it is not "PowerShell needs LF" —
# it parses either way. A Windows host's boot script is embedded into the
# instance template the same way, so its bytes are part of the template's
# identity: checked out with CRLF on one machine and LF on another, the same
# commit produces two different templates, and the pool recycles every host to
# adopt a change nobody made.
# EVERY text file, not a list of extensions. The list used to stop at the shell
# and Terraform types, so job-metadata-broker.py, turbo-cache-server.py and
# derive-turbo-tasks.cjs - embedded into host metadata by file() exactly like the
# .sh scripts - were checked out CRLF on Windows. An apply from Windows then wrote
# CRLF into all 11 host templates, and the next apply from Linux (the apply
# trigger) planned to replace every one of them back. Measured 2026-10-11:
# 11 to add, 11 to change, 11 to destroy, for a change nobody made.
# A new script type must not need a new line here to be safe.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.ps1  text eol=lf
*.tf   text eol=lf
*.hcl  text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
